/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/ A tiny "library" script meant to be imported, not run directly. `\i` (at
/ the prompt or nested in a script — see examples/README.md) compiles and runs
/ a script like `\l`, except every table/global/function it binds at its top
/ level is qualified under `.namespace_lib.*` (the file's stem) at compile
/ time, so it doesn't collide with names already in the session. Because the
/ qualification is compile-time, a library can't do `t: select from t where
/ ...` to read a session-level `t` and rebind it: a bare `t` here would always
/ mean `.namespace_lib.t`, even on the statement that first defines it.
/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

double: {[x] x*2}
greeting: "hello from namespace_lib"
lookup: select sym, price from trades where price > 100
